# Shell scripts must keep LF endings in the working tree, on every platform.
#
# This repo is developed on Windows with core.autocrlf=true, which converts
# text files to CRLF on checkout. For a .sh file that is fatal rather than
# cosmetic: the shebang becomes "#!/usr/bin/env bash\r" and Git Bash reports
#
#     bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M: No such file or directory
#
# The file that is committed is fine — the damage happens on CHECKOUT, so it
# would not show up for whoever wrote the script, only for the next person to
# clone (or for the same person after a fresh clone). Pinning eol=lf here
# overrides autocrlf for these files specifically.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Gradle wrappers are shell scripts without the extension.
gradlew text eol=lf

# PowerShell is happy with either ending, so it is left to the default.
*.ps1 text
